                <h3>What is Cybersecurity?</h3>
                <p>Protecting devices, accounts, networks, and data from digital attacks.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>In simple terms:</strong>
                  Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting your computers, phones, online accounts, and personal information from hackers, scammers, and malicious software. It covers everything from the password on your phone to the firewall on a company server.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>Example:</strong>
                  Using a strong password and two-factor authentication on your email stops attackers from reading your messages or resetting your other accounts.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Remember:</strong>
                  Cybersecurity isn't just for tech experts â€” everyone who uses the internet needs it.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="threats types attacks cyber threat">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Types of Cyber Threats</h3>
                <p>Malware, phishing, ransomware, social engineering, and more.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Main threat categories:</strong>
                  Phishing (fake messages), Malware (harmful software), Ransomware (locks your files for money), Social Engineering (psychological tricks), Man-in-the-Middle (intercepting communications), DDoS (overloading a system), and Zero-Day Exploits (attacking unknown vulnerabilities).
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>Example:</strong>
                  A fake email pretending to be from your bank is phishing. A program that secretly records your keystrokes is spyware, a type of malware.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="hacked why people get hacked mistakes human error">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Why People Get Hacked</h3>
                <p>Weak passwords, clicking bad links, reusing credentials, trusting strangers.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>The main reasons:</strong>
                  Reusing passwords across sites, clicking links without checking them, downloading files from untrusted sources, sharing personal information on social media, ignoring software updates, and falling for urgency-based scam messages.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Golden rule:</strong>
                  Pause before you click. Scammers rely on speed â€” they want you to act before you think.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="CIA triad confidentiality integrity availability">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>The CIA Triad</h3>
                <p>Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability â€” the foundation of security.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Confidentiality:</strong> Only authorized people can access data.
                  <strong>Integrity:</strong> Data hasn't been tampered with.
                  <strong>Availability:</strong> Systems and data are accessible when needed.
                  Every security decision maps back to protecting one or more of these three pillars.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>Example:</strong>
                  Encrypting your messages protects confidentiality. A checksum on a download protects integrity. A backup protects availability.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- MODULE 02 -->
        <div class="rm-module">
          <div class="rm-module-head">
            <span>// Module 02</span>
            <h2>Scams &amp; Social Engineering</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="rm-nodes">
            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="social engineering manipulation psychology trick">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>What is Social Engineering?</h3>
                <p>Manipulating people using psychology instead of hacking technology.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Social engineering attacks exploit human trust, fear, curiosity, and urgency. Attackers impersonate trusted figures such as banks, tech support, or friends to trick you into sharing information, clicking links, or sending money. No software vulnerability is needed â€” you are the target.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>Example:</strong>
                  "Your account will be closed in 24 hours unless you verify your identity now." This uses fear and urgency together.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="phishing email fake message login credential">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Phishing Attacks</h3>
                <p>Fake emails and messages that steal your login credentials.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Phishing messages pretend to be from real companies such as Netflix, Amazon, or your bank. They contain links to fake websites that look identical to real ones. When you enter your password, the attacker captures it. Always check the sender email and URL carefully.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>How to spot it:</strong>
                  Hover over links before clicking. Check for misspelled domains such as amaz0n.com or paypaI.com. Real companies never ask for your password by email.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="vishing smishing phone sms text call">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Vishing &amp; Smishing</h3>
                <p>Phone call scams and SMS text message scams.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Vishing</strong> uses phone calls. Attackers pose as bank representatives, government officials, or tech support.
                  <strong>Smishing</strong> uses text messages with malicious links.
                  Both create panic with messages such as "Your account has been compromised" or "You owe money to the government."
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Defense:</strong>
                  Never give personal information over a call you did not initiate. Hang up and call the official number directly.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="romance scam dating love fake relationship">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Romance &amp; Dating Scams</h3>
                <p>Fake relationships built online to steal money.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Scammers create attractive fake profiles on dating sites and social media. They build emotional connections over weeks or months, then ask for money while citing emergencies such as medical bills, travel costs, or business problems. They often refuse video calls and always have excuses.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Red flags:</strong>
                  They profess love very quickly, can never meet in person, repeatedly experience financial emergencies, and resist video calls.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="tech support scam fake warning popup">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Tech Support Scams</h3>
                <p>Fake virus warnings that trick you into paying for "fixes."</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Popup messages claim your computer is infected and tell you to call a number. The "technician" asks for remote access and then installs malware, steals data, or charges hundreds of dollars for fake repairs. Real companies such as Microsoft do not show browser popups asking you to call them.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>What to do:</strong>
                  Close the browser tab. Never call the displayed number. Run your own trusted antivirus scan.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="investment crypto scam money pyramid ponzi">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Investment &amp; Crypto Scams</h3>
                <p>Fake investment platforms that promise guaranteed high returns.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Scammers create professional-looking websites offering crypto trading, forex, or investment opportunities with guaranteed returns. They display fake dashboards showing growing balances. When you try to withdraw, they ask for additional fees, and your money is gone.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Rule:</strong>
                  If it sounds too good to be true, it probably is. No legitimate investment can guarantee returns. Check whether the platform is registered with a financial regulator.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="otp bank code never share personal">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>What You Should Never Share</h3>
                <p>OTPs, passwords, PINs, banking details, and ID numbers.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Never share OTP codes, passwords, PINs, bank card numbers, CVV codes, ID or passport numbers, recovery codes, or private keys. No legitimate company will ask for these through email, phone, or text.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- MODULE 03 -->
        <div class="rm-module">
          <div class="rm-module-head">
            <span>// Module 03</span>
            <h2>Malware Deep Dive</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="rm-nodes">
            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="malware what is harmful software">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>What is Malware?</h3>
                <p>Malicious software designed to damage, spy, or steal.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Malware is any software intentionally designed to cause harm. It can steal passwords, encrypt files for ransom, spy on activity, display unwanted ads, or give attackers remote control of a device. It spreads through downloads, email attachments, infected websites, and USB drives.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="virus worm spread replicate infect">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Viruses &amp; Worms</h3>
                <p>Self-replicating programs that spread across devices.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Viruses</strong> attach to legitimate files and activate when opened. They need human action to spread.
                  <strong>Worms</strong> spread automatically across networks without user interaction.
                  Both can corrupt files, slow systems, and open backdoors for additional attacks.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>Example:</strong>
                  The ILOVEYOU worm spread through email and caused billions of dollars in damage worldwide.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="trojan RAT remote access backdoor">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Trojans &amp; RATs</h3>
                <p>Malware disguised as legitimate software that opens backdoors.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Trojans</strong> pretend to be useful programs such as free games, cracked software, or fake updates, but secretly install malware.
                  <strong>RATs</strong> are Remote Access Trojans that give attackers control of a device. They may watch the screen, access the camera, steal files, and record keystrokes.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Prevention:</strong>
                  Download software only from official sources. If an expensive application is offered free by an unknown website, it may contain malware.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="ransomware encrypt files pay locked bitcoin">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Ransomware</h3>
                <p>Encrypts your files and demands payment to unlock them.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Ransomware encrypts documents, photos, and databases, then demands payment, often in cryptocurrency. Paying does not guarantee file recovery. It commonly spreads through phishing emails, infected downloads, and unpatched vulnerabilities.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Best defense:</strong>
                  Keep regular offline backups. If infected, disconnect the device, avoid paying, report the attack, and restore from a clean backup.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="spyware keylogger monitor track steal">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Spyware &amp; Keyloggers</h3>
                <p>Silently monitors everything you do and type.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Spyware</strong> monitors browsing, captures screenshots, and may track location.
                  <strong>Keyloggers</strong> record keystrokes including passwords, private messages, and payment information.
                  They are often bundled with untrusted software or installed through phishing attacks.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-example">
                  <strong>Possible signs:</strong>
                  A device becomes unusually slow, its battery drains quickly, unknown applications appear, or data usage increases without explanation.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="remove malware antivirus clean infected disconnect">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>How to Remove Malware</h3>
                <p>Step-by-step: disconnect, scan, remove, recover.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Step 1:</strong> Disconnect from the internet.
                  <strong>Step 2:</strong> Boot into Safe Mode when appropriate.
                  <strong>Step 3:</strong> Run a trusted full antivirus scan.
                  <strong>Step 4:</strong> Remove suspicious programs and files.
                  <strong>Step 5:</strong> Change passwords from a clean device.
                  <strong>Step 6:</strong> Update all software.
                  <strong>Step 7:</strong> Monitor accounts for unusual activity.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- MODULE 04 -->
        <div class="rm-module">
          <div class="rm-module-head">
            <span>// Module 04</span>
            <h2>Passwords &amp; Authentication</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="rm-nodes">
            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="strong password length symbols uppercase">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Creating Strong Passwords</h3>
                <p>Long, random, unique for every account.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  A strong password should be long and unique. Avoid personal information such as names, birthdays, or pet names. Passphrases made from several unrelated words can be strong and easier to remember.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Test:</strong>
                  If someone who knows you could easily guess it, it is not strong enough.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="password manager vault store generate">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Password Managers</h3>
                <p>Securely store and generate unique passwords for every account.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Password managers store passwords inside an encrypted vault. You remember one strong master password while the manager creates and saves unique passwords for every account. Many also warn about breached or reused passwords.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="2fa mfa authentication authenticator app sms">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Two-Factor Authentication</h3>
                <p>A second verification step that blocks unauthorized access.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Two-factor authentication adds a second layer after the password, such as a code from an authenticator application, hardware security key, or SMS message. Even if a password is stolen, the attacker still needs the second factor.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Best practice:</strong>
                  Prefer an authenticator application or hardware security key over SMS when available.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="password attack brute force dictionary credential stuffing">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Common Password Attacks</h3>
                <p>How attackers crack and steal passwords.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Brute force:</strong> Trying many possible combinations.
                  <strong>Dictionary attack:</strong> Trying common words and phrases.
                  <strong>Credential stuffing:</strong> Reusing leaked passwords from other sites.
                  <strong>Phishing:</strong> Tricking users into entering passwords on fake pages.
                  <strong>Keylogging:</strong> Recording keystrokes.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Defense:</strong>
                  Use a unique password for every account and enable two-factor authentication.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- MODULE 05 -->
        <div class="rm-module">
          <div class="rm-module-head">
            <span>// Module 05</span>
            <h2>Safe Browsing &amp; Networks</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="rm-nodes">
            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="https ssl tls encryption secure website padlock">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>HTTPS &amp; Encryption</h3>
                <p>The padlock icon and what it actually means.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  HTTPS encrypts information between your browser and a website. However, HTTPS alone does not mean the website is legitimate because scam websites can also obtain certificates. Always inspect the domain name as well.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="public wifi danger airport cafe hotel network">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Public Wi-Fi Dangers</h3>
                <p>Why coffee shop Wi-Fi can be a trap.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  On public Wi-Fi, attackers may attempt to intercept unencrypted traffic, capture credentials, inject malicious content, or redirect users to fake websites. Avoid banking or sensitive account activity on untrusted networks.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Stay safe:</strong>
                  Use mobile data for sensitive activity. When public Wi-Fi is necessary, use encrypted websites and a trusted VPN.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="vpn virtual private network tunnel encrypted">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>VPNs Explained</h3>
                <p>An encrypted tunnel that protects your internet traffic.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  A VPN encrypts internet traffic between your device and the VPN provider's server. It can protect traffic on public Wi-Fi and reduce local network visibility, but it does not make unsafe websites safe or replace antivirus protection.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="evil twin fake wifi hotspot network">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Evil Twin Attacks</h3>
                <p>Fake Wi-Fi networks that look like the real thing.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Attackers create Wi-Fi networks with names similar or identical to legitimate networks. When users connect, the attacker may monitor or manipulate traffic.
                </div>

                <div class="rm-tip">
                  <strong>Defense:</strong>
                  Ask staff for the exact network name, disable automatic connection to open networks, and use a trusted VPN when appropriate.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- MODULE 06 -->
        <div class="rm-module">
          <div class="rm-module-head">
            <span>// Module 06</span>
            <h2>Privacy &amp; Data Protection</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="rm-nodes">
            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="personal data information name phone location photos">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>What is Personal Data?</h3>
                <p>Name, phone, email, location, photos, passwords, and more.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Personal data includes information that can identify or describe you, such as your name, phone number, email address, home address, location, photos, birth date, government ID numbers, financial information, biometric data, and browsing history.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="app permissions camera microphone contacts location">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>App Permissions</h3>
                <p>Why a calculator shouldn't need your microphone.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Every permission grants an application access to part of your device or data. Review access to the camera, microphone, contacts, location, storage, and phone features. Grant only permissions required for the application's real purpose.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="data breach leaked exposed password email haveibeenpwned">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Data Breaches</h3>
                <p>When companies lose your data to hackers.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  A data breach occurs when unauthorized people obtain information stored by an organization. Exposed data may include email addresses, passwords, names, and financial details. Change affected passwords and never reuse the same password across websites.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="digital footprint social media posting online trace">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Your Digital Footprint</h3>
                <p>Everything you do online leaves a trace.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Posts, searches, likes, purchases, and account activity contribute to your digital footprint. Review privacy settings, avoid publishing sensitive personal details, and periodically inspect what information about you is publicly available.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- MODULE 07 -->
        <div class="rm-module">
          <div class="rm-module-head">
            <span>// Module 07</span>
            <h2>Prevention &amp; Response</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="rm-nodes">
            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="checklist daily safe habits routine security">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Daily Security Checklist</h3>
                <p>Simple habits that prevent most attacks.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>Daily:</strong> Avoid suspicious links and lock devices.
                  <strong>Weekly:</strong> Review permissions and install updates.
                  <strong>Monthly:</strong> Review account activity and breach alerts.
                  <strong>Always:</strong> Use unique passwords, enable two-factor authentication, keep backups, and think before sharing information.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="hacked what to do account compromised stolen">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>What To Do If Hacked</h3>
                <p>Immediate steps to contain the damage.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  <strong>1.</strong> Change passwords, beginning with your main email account.
                  <strong>2.</strong> Enable two-factor authentication.
                  <strong>3.</strong> Sign out of unknown devices and sessions.
                  <strong>4.</strong> Check for unauthorized transactions.
                  <strong>5.</strong> Scan devices for malware.
                  <strong>6.</strong> Notify your bank if financial information was exposed.
                  <strong>7.</strong> Warn contacts if your accounts were used to send scams.
                  <strong>8.</strong> Report the incident.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="report cyber crime police authorities FBI IC3">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Reporting Cyber Crime</h3>
                <p>Where and how to report attacks and fraud.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Report phishing to the organization being impersonated and scams to the relevant local cybercrime authority. Save screenshots, emails, phone numbers, web addresses, and transaction records as evidence.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="rm-node glass" data-keywords="updates software patch vulnerability safe">
              <div class="rm-node-info">
                <h3>Staying Updated &amp; Safe</h3>
                <p>Software updates close the doors that hackers use.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="rm-node-expand">
                <div class="rm-lesson">
                  Software updates repair known vulnerabilities. Enable automatic updates for operating systems, browsers, applications, and security tools. Use reputable security software and remain aware of changing scam techniques.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
